.TH VSPRITETEST 1
.SH NAME
vspritetest \- check vector sprites against the rasters extracted from them
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B vspritetest
[
.B -v
] [
.B -d
] [
.I name ...
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Vspritetest
renders each sprite in
.B /lib/temple/sprites
through
.IR vsprite (2)
and compares the pixels with the
.B .bit
that
.B scripts/tos_extract_sprites.py
rasterised from the same element list.
Two independent rasterisers, one in Python and one in Limbo, reading one
source of truth.
.PP
The comparison is per pixel, on palette indices, over the rectangle each
side says it occupies, so a sprite that came out the right shape in the
wrong place fails on the rectangle before a pixel is looked at.
.PP
The
.B .mask
is read as well as the
.BR .bit ,
and it is not optional: the extractor leaves a transparent pixel as byte
0 and CMAP8 0 is also
.BR BLACK ,
so the image alone cannot tell "nothing drew" from "drew in black".
Comparing without it reported 457 of 527 pixels differing on a sprite
that was in fact exact.
.PP
With no names it runs every sprite that has a
.B .bit
beside it and contains no element it cannot compare.
Those are named in BUGS, and the number skipped is printed, so the
coverage claim is the number of files actually compared.
.PP
It then renders each of the same sprites a second time on a 680x530 page
with the origin at its centre \(em
.IR temple / spriteedit (1)'s
canvas \(em crops that back to the sprite's box and compares.
Nothing here runs the editor.
What ties the editor to it is that
.IR temple / spriteedit (1)'s
.B replay
is a single call to
.B canvas
with exactly these arguments, which is structural rather than measured;
what is measured is that the canvas makes no difference.
A difference is reported only if the page render also put ink outside the
box, which means a flood fill escaped the sprite; a sprite that differs
inside the box while drawing nothing outside it is the two disagreeing,
and fails.
Pixels that fall off the page are counted separately: the editor cannot
show that part of the sprite, which is not the rasteriser's doing.
.PP
It then checks the transform, which has no
.B .bit
to be checked against, by three properties that hold whatever the right
answer is \(em turning by nothing and scaling by one changes nothing,
four quarter turns come back, and scaling by four and then by a quarter
is the original \(em compared as pixels rather than as arithmetic.
All three are satisfied by a transform that does nothing, so a fourth
requires that scaling by four produces at least twice the ink.
The measure is ink and not the bounding box: the box carries the pen's
half-width as a margin and the pen does not scale unless the sprite sets
a thickness, so a 7-pixel-wide sprite of two lines 4 apart comes out 19
wide and not 28.
Sprites containing a
.B rect
are skipped, for the reason in
.IR vsprite (2)'s
BUGS.
.PP
.B -v
names each sprite that matches exactly.
.B -d
prints a window of got over want around the first difference, as palette
indices in hex with
.B .
for transparent \(em a shift shows as a row sliding, a colour error as
one digit changing everywhere.
.SH EXAMPLE
.EX
; emu-g -r. /dis/vspritetest.dis
vspritetest: 166 sprites skipped (text, bitmap or mesh)
near elephant_5: 8 of 3675 pixels differ (allowed 16), first at 2,36
  (three more)
vspritetest: transform 46/46, 11 skipped for a rect
vspritetest: page 57/57 identical inside the box, 0 flooded outside it, 3 cut off by the page
vspritetest: 57/57
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/vspritetest.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR vsprite (2),
.IR temple (1)
.SH BUGS
Exact is the bar and 52 of the 57 meet it.
The other five are allowed sixteen pixels, or a ten-thousandth of the
sprite if that is more, and the count is printed whenever it is not zero
so that it cannot grow unnoticed.
Those five are sprites whose shape comes out of
.I cos
and
.IR sin :
the extractor is Python against its libm and this is Limbo against
.IR math (2),
the two disagree in the last bits, and a point that lands either side of
a half moves one pixel.
Neither side can be argued into the other.
.PP
A display is asked for when there is no draw context, because
.I vsprite
draws no text at all without a font and that reads as a font that
disagrees rather than as one that is missing.
It is what made
.B spriteplot_1
look 387 pixels wrong when it is 20.
.PP
Sprites containing
.BR text ,
.BR textbox ,
.BR textdiamond ,
.BR bitmap ,
.B mesh
or
.B shiftmesh
are skipped: the first three go through a different font on each side,
and the rest are not drawn by
.IR vsprite (2)
at all.
.PP
.B floodfillnot
is skipped for a different reason, and the reason is in the extractor.
Its
.I flood
takes an invert flag and never uses it, so the
.B .bit
for such a sprite records a plain fill.
Matching it would mean copying the omission rather than drawing the
element.
.PP
That leaves 166 of the 248 sprites uncompared.
Nothing here says the ones with text or bitmaps in them render correctly.
Naming one on the command line compares it anyway, and under a display
that is worth doing:
.B spriteplot_1
differs by 20 of 19,125 pixels, which is over the allowance but is the
same shape-from-cos-and-sin disagreement as the five that are inside it,
not the font.
